WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.640
Two small cells. One is a methane-making microbe, a methanogen.

2
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.760
The other is the bacterium Escherichia coli.

3
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.680
Under a microscope they look much alike, and neither has a nucleus.

4
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.940
Methanogens live in the guts of cattle and humans.

5
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:25.920
Are they close relatives?

6
00:00:26.883 --> 00:00:33.283
For a long time, biologists lumped cells with no nucleus together as prokaryotes.

7
00:00:34.063 --> 00:00:36.243
That grouping rests on an absence.

8
00:00:37.163 --> 00:00:41.043
Two cells that both lack a nucleus need not be close relatives.

9
00:00:42.303 --> 00:00:46.063
Every cell has ribosomes, the particles that make proteins.

10
00:00:47.383 --> 00:00:50.423
Carl Woese looked for a part that every cell carries.

11
00:00:50.883 --> 00:00:54.523
He chose the RNA of the small ribosomal subunit.

12
00:00:54.523 --> 00:00:59.003
It is present in all cells, and it does the same job in each.

13
00:00:59.883 --> 00:01:03.543
His laboratory and its collaborators grew microbes.

14
00:01:04.423 --> 00:01:09.363
They included typical bacteria and several methane-producing species, called

15
00:01:09.363 --> 00:01:16.103
methanogens. They extracted the ribosomal RNA and cut it into short pieces with an

16
00:01:16.103 --> 00:01:18.643
enzyme, ribonuclease T1.

17
00:01:19.683 --> 00:01:22.243
Then they catalogued the sequence of each piece.

18
00:01:23.867 --> 00:01:27.927
Each pair of organisms then received a similarity score.

19
00:01:28.587 --> 00:01:32.047
The score was higher when their catalogues shared more pieces.

20
00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:39.127
Under the old split, methanogens look like bacteria, so they should score closest to

21
00:01:39.127 --> 00:01:41.287
other bacteria. They did not.

22
00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:50.230
In 1977, Woese and George Fox reported that the methanogens formed their own group.

23
00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:56.930
It was about as distant from typical bacteria as from the cytoplasmic ribosomal RNA

24
00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:03.590
of eukaryotes. They called it the archaebacteria, a third primary kingdom.

25
00:02:04.433 --> 00:02:12.413
In 1990, Woese, Otto Kandler and Mark Wheelis proposed the three domains now in use.

26
00:02:13.273 --> 00:02:16.913
They are Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya.

27
00:02:18.253 --> 00:02:21.453
Archaea differ from bacteria in more than RNA.

28
00:02:22.393 --> 00:02:27.313
Their membrane lipids have ether bonds, and their walls lack peptidoglycan.

29
00:02:27.973 --> 00:02:30.513
They are not simply extreme bacteria.

30
00:02:31.373 --> 00:02:37.373
The comparison observed no ancestor, and it did not show how eukaryotic cells arose.

31
00:02:38.233 --> 00:02:43.653
It did show that appearance does not reveal deep relationships, and that a shared

32
00:02:43.653 --> 00:02:44.753
molecule can.
